# rlwitwe.msg - Loretta Varga, widow of the Silver Garter's last owner (New Reno)
# Plain ASCII only: the English Fallout 2 fonts have no umlauts or typographic quotes.

{100}{}{(A woman in black sits among dust sheets in what used to be the bridal suite. She doesn't get up.) You're here about the hotel. They all come about the hotel. Nobody comes about me.}
{101}{}{I want to buy the deed.}
{102}{}{[Speech] What are you going to do with a hotel nobody in this town dares to open?}
{103}{}{Where is the deed now?}
{104}{}{Goodbye, Mrs. Varga.}
{105}{}{Hotel?}

# Buying
{110}{}{A thousand. That's what my husband owed the Salvatores the night they shot him on the front steps. I'd like to pay it back and never hear that name again.}
{111}{}{Here's a thousand.}
{112}{}{[Barter] Seven fifty, and the debt goes with the house. Nobody knocks on your door again.}
{113}{}{I don't have that much.}
{114}{}{(She counts it twice. Then she takes a folded paper from her dress and puts it on the table without looking at it.) Forty years. And it fits in an envelope.}
{115}{}{(She thinks about it for a long time.) Seven fifty. And if anyone asks about the debt, you send them to yourself. (She puts a folded paper on the table.) Take it before I change my mind.}
{116}{}{Thank you, Mrs. Varga.}

# Talked into it
{120}{}{(She laughs, and it sounds like it hurts.) Nothing. I'll sit here until the roof comes down. (She takes a folded paper from her dress.) Take it. If somebody opens this place again, maybe they'll stop looking at me like I'm the one they buried.}
{121}{}{I'll make it worth something.}

# The grave
{130}{}{The deed? (Her mouth goes thin.) In my husband's breast pocket. He always said a Varga doesn't sell. So he took it with him. Golgotha, third row from the wall, the one with the brass plate. I paid for the plate. Nobody else did.}
{131}{}{[Leave. Tonight you go to Golgotha with a shovel.]}
{132}{}{Let's talk about something else.}
{135}{}{(Golgotha after midnight. The brass plate is where she said. The ground is soft; nobody has visited him. The deed is in his breast pocket, folded twice, the ink barely faded. When you come back to the Garter at dawn, the widow's chair is empty.)}
{136}{}{(A gravedigger with a lantern saw you climb out of the hole. By noon half of Virgin Street will know what the new owner of the Garter does at night.)}
{137}{}{...}

# The forgery is found out
{200}{}{(The widow is back in her chair, and this time she's looking at you.) You forged his name. His name was the only thing he left me, and you forged it on paper from the Golden Globes. Five hundred. Or I tell everyone on Virgin Street what the Garter stands on.}
{201}{}{Here's five hundred. We're done.}
{202}{}{Tell whoever you like.}
{203}{}{No.}
{210}{}{(She takes the money without counting it.) Now we're both thieves. (She gets up and leaves, and she doesn't look back.)}
{211}{}{(She nods slowly, as if you've confirmed something.) Then I'll tell them. (She leaves. By evening the families know.)}
{212}{}{...}

# Look
{300}{}{An old woman in black, sitting very straight.}
{301}{}{Loretta Varga. Her husband owned the Silver Garter until the Salvatores collected a debt on the front steps.}
